{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-70832" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the straight line with the canonical equation $\\frac{x-3}{2}=\\frac{y-1}{1}=\\frac{z+4}{3}.$ Find the vector equation of the line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/state.json" "file_sha256" "570dda02acd57c60b6e215edeae92ca8ed4b949c6322d76e5af575968de3ade1"} {"question" "q-70832" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{2}=\\frac{y-1}{1}=\\frac{z-(-4)}{3},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(3,1,-4)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨2,1,3⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/state.json" "file_sha256" "570dda02acd57c60b6e215edeae92ca8ed4b949c6322d76e5af575968de3ade1"} {"question" "q-70832" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "080ba4d1769da3809cbf2ab4e44f58784d7dd89caeba612e18774fa2b95f178e"} {"question" "q-70832" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file"